cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  AI and strategic behaviour in repeated price competition: an imitation
  mechanism and a panel of language models on shared opponents
message: >-
  If you use this repository, its harnesses or its match data, please cite it as
  below. The run conditions that make the language-model data interpretable are
  in llm/results/README.md and must be reported alongside any reuse.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Mohamed Ali Tewfik
    family-names: Hamlil
    email: mohamed.hamlil@etu.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr
    affiliation: >-
      Université Grenoble Alpes, UFR IM²AG
repository-code: https://github.com/Tewf/IA-Economie-Strategique
url: https://tewf.github.io/IA-Economie-Strategique/
abstract: >-
  In repeated price competition human players tend to settle on tacitly
  collusive prices rather than the competitive equilibrium, and whether
  algorithms sustain that behaviour, break it or intensify it is an open
  question with direct consequences for competition policy. This repository
  continues a research internship at GAEL (Université Grenoble Alpes and INRAE,
  January to April 2025, supervised by Alexis Garapin and Olivier Bonroy) by
  running the two mechanisms the internship report proposed but did not
  implement, against one set of opponents and one measure of reciprocity.

  The first is a Hebbian imitation agent, which the report expected Tit-for-Tat
  to emerge from. It does not: over matches of 10 to 100 turns the agent
  finishes eighth of eight against seven opponents from the literature, behind a
  coin flip, because the update it implements is frequency matching whose state
  cannot depend on the last round. In self-play the mechanism is a ratchet on
  the initial condition, locking onto whichever regime it starts in over 700
  runs out of 700.

  The second is homo silicus, cited in the report's conclusion and never run:
  five open-weight language models of 4B to 8B parameters, executed locally and
  offline, playing the same iterated Prisoner's Dilemma against each other and
  against the same opponents, with and without a non-binding cheap-talk channel,
  from imposed cooperative, imposed defective and neutral openings. Over 220
  matches of 30 rounds, three of the four readable models reproduce the
  imitator's ratchet exactly, defecting in every round of an imposed defective
  opening; a non-binding message frees exactly one of those three and leaves the
  other two unchanged, while a fourth model escapes the same opening with no
  channel at all. Communication is therefore neither necessary nor sufficient
  for leaving an imposed regime, and the ability to leave one is a property of
  the individual model rather than of language models.
keywords:
  - tacit collusion
  - iterated prisoner's dilemma
  - cheap talk
  - large language models
  - homo silicus
  - multi-agent systems
  - behavioural economics
  - reciprocity
